Exercise 1: Quantitative vs Quantitative Intuition Check



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(elections, aes(y = repub_pct_20, x = repub_pct_16)) +
  geom_point()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](ica-bi_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-2-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::



Exercise 2: 2 Quantititative Variables



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Set up the plotting frame
# How does this differ than the frame for our histogram of repub_pct_20 alone?
ggplot(elections, aes(y = repub_pct_20, x = repub_pct_16))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](ica-bi_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-3-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Add a layer of points for each county
# Take note of the geom!
ggplot(elections, aes(y = repub_pct_20, x = repub_pct_16)) +
  geom_point()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](ica-bi_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){width=672}
