Exercise 12: Categorical vs Categorical



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# A stacked bar plot
# How are the "historical" and "winner_20" variables mapped to the plot, i.e. what roles do they play?
ggplot(elections, aes(x = historical, fill = winner_20)) +
  geom_bar()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](ica-bi_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-24-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# A faceted bar plot
ggplot(elections, aes(x = winner_20)) +
  geom_bar() +
  facet_wrap(~ historical)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](ica-bi_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-25-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# A side-by-side bar plot
# Note the new argument to geom_bar
ggplot(elections, aes(x = historical, fill = winner_20)) +
  geom_bar(position = "dodge")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](ica-bi_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-26-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# A proportional bar plot
# Note the new argument to geom_bar
ggplot(elections, aes(x = historical, fill = winner_20)) +
  geom_bar(position = "fill")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](ica-bi_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-27-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::




Part a
> pro = easier to compare the relative outcomes in blue vs purple vs red states con = lose track of how many counties fall into blue vs purple vs red states

Part b
> I like the last bar plot because it reveals the percentarge  of dem and repub in a clear way.

